    <TD align=3Dmiddle>
      <H3>Red-legged partridge</H3>
      <DIV><IMG height=3D158 alt=3D""=20
      =
src=3D"http://www.kurdistanica.com/english/culture/ncharacters/cha-images=
/redlegged_partridge.jpg"=20
      width=3D180 align=3Dleft></DIV>
      <P>Red-legged partridge with scientific name "Alectoris rufa" =
calls "Kew,=20
      kev, Kewk, kev=ED, and kevk=ED" in Kurdish. Red-legged partridge =
is normally=20
      thirteen inches in length, but does not mention either its breadth =
or=20
      weight. The bill, legs, and orbits are red; the irides hazel; chin =
and=20
      throat dull white, surrounded by a black line or streak, which =
passes from=20
      the brow and nostrils to the eyes, behind which it continues, =
falls down=20
      before the auriculars, and meets on the lower part of the neck: a =
white=20
      streak extends from the brow over the eyes towards the hinder part =
of the=20
      neck; the forehead is bluish ash, fading into a rusty chesnut =
behind,=20
      where the feathers are somewhat elongated, and can be erected or =
depressed=20
      at pleasure; the hinder part of the neck is rusty chesnut, formed =
into a=20
      stripe, which falls down towards the shoulders; the rest of the =
neck is=20
      pale ash, inclining to blue, with numerous longish spots of black; =
below=20
      this, to the shoulders and breast, the plumage is rusty brown, =
with a=20
      vinous reflection; the belly and thighs pale rusty chesnut; the =
breast=20
      pale lead grey; sides beautifully ornamented with stripes composed =
of=20
      black, bright reddish chesnut, and white; the upper parts of the =
plumage=20
      are rufous brown, somewhat tinged with olive grey; quills nearly =
the same,=20
      but darker, the outer edges yellowish; vent brownish ash: the tail =

      consists of sixteen feathers, the middle ones greyish brown, the =
outer=20
      ones deep reddish chesnut; under coverts of the tail nearly the =
same: the=20
      legs are furnished with a kind of double knob instead of a =
spur.</P>
      <DIV align=3Dright><IMG height=3D232 hspace=3D10=20
      =
src=3D"http://www.kurdistanica.com/english/culture/ncharacters/cha-images=
/redlegged_partridge2.jpg"=20
      width=3D350 align=3Dright vspace=3D10></DIV>
      <P>These birds are found in various parts of Kurdistan; and are =
common in=20
      Germany, France, and Italy; the islands of Madeira, Guernsey and =
Jersey;=20
      but the accounts of their breeding in England, are contradictory. =
In a=20
      wild state, they prefer woody and heathy wastes, to in closed =
ground, but=20
      they are easily tamed, and soon become offensively familiar.=20
      <P>&nbsp;</P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE>
